  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    ---
    
    *Pressed-lichen sheet, stained with mineral residue along the left margin. Sealed with standard tunnel-wax, charcoal-black. Carried by Splitpath.*
    
    **Third ring, ☽'slew Nisaemesius~. Forty-one days past Accord.**
    
    Otrolal—
    
    The light I write by costs something, and tonight I am paying more than usual. Three fire-cores on the desk: two steady, one flickering in a way that reminds me of the vein we lost below the sixth. You were still pulling guard-rotation at the lower chambers when Aetunre☽ brought the survey numbers. I won't repeat them here. Splitpath carries enough weight past the seventeen already.
    
    Accord was what it always is. I stood behind the fire-core table with my guild-face on, measured speech for the crystal-forgers, clipped efficiency for us. The empty stall at the Promenade's western edge—still empty. Someone left the container again. The surface merchants are formal, careful, and their aurora-crystals came in twelve crates fewer than promised. I asked the Stack delegation about shortfall. They talked about sky conditions. I listened to what they didn't say underneath, which was: *we don't trust the routes anymore.*
    
    Our friends who meet in rotating rooms have been quieter since the Claws took what they took. Not grieving—grinding. The owl's latest opinion, as near as I can read it, is patience. You know what Nightshelf patience looks like from below.
    
    I handled six aurora-crystals during the exchange. My flippers did not shake. I am telling you this because you will ask, and because I would rather the answer travel by Splitpath's hand than wait for the look you give me when you think I don't notice.
    
    The cracks in the ceiling above the second ring are wider. Velumium~ says ice-crystal patch. I say ice-crystal is already cracking in the fourth. The old proverb about sealing one tunnel to flood the next comes to mind, but the proverb doesn't quite fit anymore, does it? Nothing quite fits.
    
    Write when the patrol allows. I sealed this with the usual wax. You know who carried it.
    
    —☽'Cilocum
    
    ---
    
    *Scraped hide, small. Cramped hand—written by diminished fire-core, single source. Sealed with tunnel-wax, smudged. Carried by Downdraft.*
    
    **Lower passages, south-spiral. Eleven days after receipt.**
    
    Cilocum—
    
    Received. Hide is short tonight so I will be shorter.
    
    The survey numbers reached me through other channels. You are right not to write them. The veins at the sixth are not unstable, they are *finished*, and the difference between those words is the difference between a plan and a funeral. Aetunre☽ knows. Whether Aetunre☽ reports what Aetunre☽ knows is a separate arithmetic.
    
    I noticed the crystal-count. Twelve crates fewer is not sky conditions. Twelve crates fewer is someone on the Stack side deciding our warming-months are negotiable. The Accord held in name. The Accord always holds in name.
    
    The owl-friends grinding in dark rooms—let them grind. They won't retake what they lost; the joke they tell about winning a war they lost stopped being funny two cycles ago, though I respect that they still polish the old amulets. There is something honest about tending a fire that's already gone out. You would know.
    
    Six crystals and steady flippers. Good. I won't ask again until next time.
    
    Something watches the patrol routes below the fourth depth. I have logged it in the usual notation: presence, edge-of-sight, patient. Not orca. Not ours. The cold down there is not weather, it is geology, and the geology has opinions. I file this under the same heading as always: *noted, unanswered.*
    
    Your proverbs are slipping, old teacher. When the saying doesn't fit, it's not the saying that's changed.
    
    The ice has a longer memory than either of us. Mind what you're feeding it.
    
    —Otrolal
    
    *[Ancestor-bone clasp impression pressed into the wax.]*
    
    ---
    
    *Pressed lichen, clean, edges trimmed with care. Faint scent of fire-core oil. Sealed twice. Carried by Ferryman—last route logged before the deep-passage closure. Dated nine months after the previous letter. The gap is unexplained.*
    
    **South-spiral, near the Fracta threshold.**
    
    Cilocum—
    
    I have given the lower-warmth cavern patrol logs to Keeper ☽'Vacemtucis'Tier with instructions that are not instructions. You will understand when you read them. The maps of the sixth depth are in the usual place. The maps of what is below the sixth are in the other usual place, which I trust you have not forgotten, though you have forgotten other things lately that a Depth-Purveyor should not forget.
    
    The dagger fits a different hand now. I am not speaking in proverb. ☽'micseleia drinks the light down here the same way it always has, but the hand it needs next is not mine and we both know whose it isn't, either. Someone will come for it. They always do.
    
    I did not go to see about the horizon. I went to see about the door that opens when the living stop following. There is a difference, though I accept you may read this too late to appreciate it.
    
    Your flippers. Mind your flippers around the crystals. The light you write by costs something and you have been overpaying for years.
    
    I sealed this with the usual wax. You know who carried it.
    
    —Otrolal
    
    *[No clasp impression.]*
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771588990924_0", anchor: "I did not go to see about the horizon", text: "The horizon is what the Walking's victims seek — that compulsion that seizes penguins who have lost much or suffered greatly, pulling them out onto the ice toward nothing. Otrolal's explicit rejection of the horizon is what unsettles me. The Walking is not supposed to permit this clarity. Those it takes do not write farewell letters distinguishing between one departure and another. Either Otrolal resisted the compulsion long enough to choose a different threshold, or what claimed them was not the Walking at all. The nine-month gap. The absent clasp. I do not know which possibility is worse.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588990924_1", anchor: "the door that opens when the living stop following", text: "Not proverb. During the Long Dark the boundary between living world and ice-memory thins — shadow-walkers can travel farther but risk losing themselves. Otrolal's 'door' is the liminal threshold where passage replaces perception. Nightshelf operational records use nearly identical phrasing.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588990924_2", anchor: "presence, edge-of-sight, patient. Not orca. Not ours", text: "Otrolal has a filing system for encounters with pre-penguin presences. 'Noted, unanswered.' How extraordinary that someone developed administrative notation for the unknowable. The deep ice contains impressions from before any penguin civilization existed — I have seen them at Foundation depth, and 'patient' is precisely the word I would choose. That Otrolal logs these alongside patrol data, in the same professional register, strikes me as either the sanest or the most deranged response possible.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588990924_3", anchor: "you have been overpaying for years", text: "Not for fire-cores. ☽'Cilocum's shaking flippers around aurora-crystals, the light that 'costs something' — this is corruption exposure. Prolonged handling of trade goods passing through contaminated ice without adequate cleansing. I noted in ☽'Cilocum's entry that the dimmed amulets tell a specific story. Otrolal is reading the same story here and saying goodbye to the person inside it.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
